1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,600
A wet morning in Manhattan.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:05,837
Above the traffic, strangers
rent desks, only by the month,

3
00:00:05,904 --> 00:00:07,970
in an office not one of them owns.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:13,639
The coffee is free, there's a beer tap in
the lobby, and a sign on the wall says:

5
00:00:13,706 --> 00:00:14,820
do what you love.

6
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:18,472
Adam Neumann's WeWork was
an office company that told

7
00:00:18,539 --> 00:00:22,460
investors its mission was to
elevate the world's consciousness.

8
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:25,541
This is the story of
how that company fell,

9
00:00:25,608 --> 00:00:28,010
and how its founder walked out richer.

10
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,833
And here's the stranger
question underneath the money.

11
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:33,120
How do you raise money for your next

12
00:00:33,187 --> 00:00:36,680
company before the first one
has even finished falling?

13
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:41,253
So we'll keep two accounts side by side:
the company's, and the founder's.

14
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:45,370
Keep an eye on which one pays the
bills, and which one gets paid.

15
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,536
In August twenty twenty-two,
Andreessen Horowitz,

16
00:00:48,603 --> 00:00:51,920
one of Silicon Valley's most
famous investors, made a bet.

17
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:54,873
About three hundred and
fifty million dollars,

18
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:57,523
into Adam Neumann's new
housing company, Flow.

19
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:01,470
It was reported as the biggest single
check the firm had ever paid out.

20
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:05,780
But WeWork only filed for
bankruptcy fifteen months later.

21
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:09,560
The comeback started before
the crash had even finished.

22
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:13,836
Here's how that happened, and the
one decision, signed years earlier,

23
00:01:13,903 --> 00:01:15,150
that made it possible.

24
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:16,803
It began in twenty ten,

25
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:20,777
when Adam Neumann and Miguel McKelvey
started a company called WeWork.

26
00:01:21,207 --> 00:01:25,307
They had just sold their first shared
office business, GreenDesk, in Brooklyn.

27
00:01:25,737 --> 00:01:30,197
Their first floor, at one fifty-four Grand
Street in SoHo, opened in twenty eleven.

28
00:01:30,627 --> 00:01:33,317
The trick was simple. Rent
a whole floor for years.

29
00:01:33,747 --> 00:01:36,767
Then cut it into desks,
and rent the desks out by the month.

30
00:01:37,197 --> 00:01:40,507
Add coffee, glass walls and a
beer tap. Call it a community.

31
00:01:40,937 --> 00:01:42,546
For a member, that meant freedom:

32
00:01:42,613 --> 00:01:46,017
you could leave the office any
month you liked, no questions asked.

33
00:01:46,447 --> 00:01:50,750
But for WeWork, a lease was a promise to
pay rent for years, whatever happened.

34
00:01:50,817 --> 00:01:53,457
Remember that. It's the whole story.

35
00:01:53,887 --> 00:01:55,407
Then the money arrived, from Tokyo.

36
00:01:56,087 --> 00:01:59,332
In August twenty seventeen,
SoftBank, run by Masayoshi Son,

37
00:01:59,399 --> 00:02:01,757
announced four point four billion dollars.

38
00:02:02,187 --> 00:02:04,267
But only three billion
went into WeWork itself.

39
00:02:04,687 --> 00:02:07,767
The rest went to its offices in
China, Japan and the Pacific.

40
00:02:08,207 --> 00:02:10,530
So growth became the only
number investors measured.

41
00:02:10,597 --> 00:02:15,103
WeWork's desks went from about one hundred
thousand, at the end of twenty sixteen,

42
00:02:15,170 --> 00:02:18,237
to four hundred and sixty-six
thousand two years later.

43
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:22,278
In January twenty nineteen, SoftBank
came back with two billion more,

44
00:02:22,345 --> 00:02:24,797
at a valuation of
forty-seven billion dollars.

45
00:02:25,227 --> 00:02:27,970
But only one billion of that
was new money for the company.

46
00:02:28,037 --> 00:02:31,157
The other billion bought shares
from people who already owned them.

47
00:02:31,587 --> 00:02:34,440
So forty-seven billion
dollars wasn't a market price.

48
00:02:34,507 --> 00:02:38,207
It was a price set by the very
investor who was paying for the growth,

49
00:02:38,274 --> 00:02:39,297
with its own money.

50
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:42,740
Meanwhile, the founder's account
was quietly filling with money.

51
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:46,027
Neumann's own company held
the rights to the word "we".

52
00:02:46,094 --> 00:02:49,230
So WeWork paid it five point
nine million dollars for them.

53
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:51,644
The company's own filing admits he had

54
00:02:51,711 --> 00:02:54,140
owned stakes in buildings
that WeWork rented.

55
00:02:54,570 --> 00:02:58,000
So in some buildings, the founder
was his own company's landlord.

56
00:02:58,430 --> 00:02:59,989
In April twenty nineteen,

57
00:03:00,056 --> 00:03:03,891
WeWork lent him three hundred
and sixty-two million dollars,

58
00:03:03,958 --> 00:03:07,210
at two point eight nine
percent, to buy its stock.

59
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:11,353
By that July, he had taken out
about seven hundred million dollars.

60
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:15,960
Share sales, and loans against his stock,
the Wall Street Journal reported.

61
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:18,762
The company also owned a Gulfstream jet,

62
00:03:18,829 --> 00:03:21,450
bought for more than
sixty million dollars.

63
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,897
But here's the problem with
a private account: one day,

64
00:03:24,964 --> 00:03:26,590
someone else gets to read it.

65
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:31,833
On Wednesday, August fourteenth,
twenty nineteen, WeWork filed its S-1.

66
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,900
It's the document a company must publish

67
00:03:33,967 --> 00:03:36,190
before it sells shares
on the stock market.

68
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:38,340
Outsiders could finally read it.

69
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:39,721
In twenty eighteen,

70
00:03:39,788 --> 00:03:43,100
WeWork brought in one point eight
two billion dollars of revenue.

71
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:45,490
And it lost one point six one billion.

72
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:50,535
So the filing invented its own profit
measure: "community-adjusted EBITDA".

73
00:03:50,602 --> 00:03:52,343
It left out costs like rent.

74
00:03:52,410 --> 00:03:55,023
For a company whose whole
business was paying rent,

75
00:03:55,090 --> 00:03:57,350
that's like a bakery
leaving out the flour.

76
00:03:57,780 --> 00:04:01,530
Then, on page forty-one of the filing,
one line decided everything.

77
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:06,570
Future lease payments, already signed:
forty-seven point two billion dollars.

78
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,070
Against that, contracted membership
revenue of about four billion.

79
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,820
Years of rent on one side. Months
of membership on the other.

80
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:18,449
That's the poison pill: rent
for years, sell by the month,

81
00:04:18,516 --> 00:04:20,430
and let your funder set the price.

82
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:22,517
It also showed who was in charge.

83
00:04:22,584 --> 00:04:24,830
Neumann's shares carried
twenty votes each.

84
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:29,320
And if the founder died, his wife
Rebekah would help choose the next CEO.

85
00:04:29,750 --> 00:04:30,915
So investors read it,

86
00:04:30,982 --> 00:04:35,560
and the forty-seven-billion price tag
fell toward fifteen, then as low as ten.

87
00:04:35,990 --> 00:04:39,560
In September, WeWork cut his votes,
dropped the successor clause,

88
00:04:39,627 --> 00:04:41,810
and he handed back the trademark money.

89
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:45,933
But it wasn't enough. On Tuesday,
September twenty-fourth,

90
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:47,880
Adam Neumann resigned as CEO.

91
00:04:48,310 --> 00:04:50,880
The same day, the company
put the jet up for sale.

92
00:04:51,310 --> 00:04:54,410
Six days later, the stock
market listing was cancelled.

93
00:04:54,853 --> 00:04:59,133
And that's when the two accounts
split, and the money went one way.

94
00:04:59,563 --> 00:05:02,008
In October, SoftBank rescued WeWork.

95
00:05:02,075 --> 00:05:05,556
The headline said nine and
a half billion dollars.

96
00:05:05,623 --> 00:05:08,783
But only six and a half
billion was for the company.

97
00:05:09,213 --> 00:05:13,086
Three billion was an offer to
buy shares from existing owners.

98
00:05:13,153 --> 00:05:15,493
One of them was the founder, Adam Neumann.

99
00:05:15,913 --> 00:05:19,556
His exit was reported as one
point seven billion dollars.

100
00:05:19,623 --> 00:05:23,873
In fact, about nine hundred and
seventy million was for his shares.

101
00:05:24,303 --> 00:05:27,536
Five hundred million was a
credit line, which is a loan.

102
00:05:27,603 --> 00:05:31,333
And one hundred and eighty-five
million was a consulting fee.

103
00:05:31,763 --> 00:05:34,170
Part of that deal was later cancelled,

104
00:05:34,237 --> 00:05:37,753
then settled on terms that
were never fully disclosed.

105
00:05:38,183 --> 00:05:40,823
The company kept the leases.

106
00:05:41,253 --> 00:05:45,913
Then, in twenty twenty, the pandemic
emptied the offices in every city.

107
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:50,493
But it didn't kill WeWork.
WeWork lasted three more years.

108
00:05:50,923 --> 00:05:55,946
In October twenty twenty-one, it finally
reached the stock market, through a SPAC.

109
00:05:56,013 --> 00:05:59,740
A SPAC is a shell company that's
already on the stock market,

110
00:05:59,807 --> 00:06:01,983
so a merger skips the usual filing.

111
00:06:02,413 --> 00:06:04,816
The headline value was
nine billion dollars.

112
00:06:04,883 --> 00:06:08,106
But the shares were valued at
about six and a half billion.

113
00:06:08,173 --> 00:06:11,843
And the cash it actually raised
was about one point three billion.

114
00:06:12,273 --> 00:06:16,113
A big headline, then a
smaller real number. Again.

115
00:06:16,553 --> 00:06:18,112
In March twenty twenty-three,

116
00:06:18,179 --> 00:06:22,103
WeWork announced its first month of
adjusted profit, back in December.

117
00:06:22,533 --> 00:06:23,524
Five months later,

118
00:06:23,591 --> 00:06:26,108
its own report warned
of "substantial doubt"

119
00:06:26,175 --> 00:06:28,583
that it could survive as a going concern.

120
00:06:29,013 --> 00:06:32,943
Because paying for space ate seventy-four
cents of every dollar of revenue.

121
00:06:33,373 --> 00:06:37,333
And even its buildings sat partly empty:
only seventy-two percent full.

122
00:06:37,753 --> 00:06:39,375
Its lawyers said it had already

123
00:06:39,442 --> 00:06:41,772
renegotiated five hundred
and ninety leases,

124
00:06:41,839 --> 00:06:42,983
building by building.

125
00:06:43,413 --> 00:06:48,423
Then, on Monday, November sixth, twenty
twenty-three, WeWork filed for bankruptcy.

126
00:06:48,853 --> 00:06:52,306
It had about one hundred and
sixty-four million dollars in cash.

127
00:06:52,373 --> 00:06:56,110
Against that: almost nineteen
billion in liabilities on its books,

128
00:06:56,177 --> 00:06:58,193
more than a hundred times its cash.

129
00:06:58,633 --> 00:07:02,293
In bankruptcy, a judge can do what
no founder could: cancel leases.

130
00:07:02,723 --> 00:07:06,556
The first batch was sixty-nine.
Floor by floor, office by office,

131
00:07:06,623 --> 00:07:07,943
the community emptied.

132
00:07:08,363 --> 00:07:11,663
And the founder? Fifteen
months into his next company.

133
00:07:12,093 --> 00:07:14,034
Between twenty twenty
and twenty twenty-two,

134
00:07:14,101 --> 00:07:16,133
companies he controlled
bought majority stakes

135
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:17,933
in more than four thousand apartments.

136
00:07:18,363 --> 00:07:22,233
Flow would rent them out with the same
promise: not just a home, a community.

137
00:07:22,673 --> 00:07:25,206
Andreessen Horowitz's
co-founder, Marc Andreessen,

138
00:07:25,273 --> 00:07:27,076
explained the bet in his own words.

139
00:07:27,143 --> 00:07:27,685
ANDREESSEN:

140
00:07:27,752 --> 00:07:30,177
Adam is a visionary
leader who revolutionized

141
00:07:30,244 --> 00:07:32,613
the second largest asset
class in the world,

142
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:33,953
commercial real estate.

143
00:07:34,383 --> 00:07:35,846
The day that post went up,

144
00:07:35,913 --> 00:07:38,845
investor Jason Calacanis
wrote that a startup worth

145
00:07:38,912 --> 00:07:41,853
a billion before it launches
is "probably a scam".

146
00:07:42,287 --> 00:07:46,007
In early twenty twenty-four,
the founder tried to buy WeWork back.

147
00:07:46,437 --> 00:07:50,283
His offer was reported at more
than five hundred million dollars,

148
00:07:50,350 --> 00:07:52,457
then six hundred and fifty million.

149
00:07:52,887 --> 00:07:57,037
His letter said he was partnering with
Dan Loeb's hedge fund, Third Point.

150
00:07:57,467 --> 00:08:00,147
But Third Point said it had
made no commitment to pay.

151
00:08:00,577 --> 00:08:05,520
So the creditors chose their own deal,
and in May, Neumann walked away.

152
00:08:05,587 --> 00:08:06,057
NEUMANN:

153
00:08:06,124 --> 00:08:09,618
The company looks to be
emerging from bankruptcy with

154
00:08:09,685 --> 00:08:13,447
a plan that appears unrealistic
and unlikely to succeed.

155
00:08:13,877 --> 00:08:20,030
In June twenty twenty-four, WeWork came
out of bankruptcy, owned mostly by Yardi,

156
00:08:20,097 --> 00:08:22,247
a property software company.

157
00:08:22,677 --> 00:08:27,910
Yardi paid three hundred and thirty-seven
million dollars for about sixty percent.

158
00:08:27,977 --> 00:08:30,140
SoftBank had put in, or promised,

159
00:08:30,207 --> 00:08:33,857
more than ten billion before
the stock market listing.

160
00:08:34,277 --> 00:08:35,990
Then, in April twenty twenty-five,

161
00:08:36,057 --> 00:08:38,930
Flow raised more than one
hundred million dollars more.

162
00:08:38,997 --> 00:08:41,787
The reported value:
about two and a half billion.

163
00:08:42,227 --> 00:08:46,448
So, back to that wet morning in
Manhattan. The desks are still there,

164
00:08:46,515 --> 00:08:48,877
rented by the month, under new owners.

165
00:08:49,307 --> 00:08:51,780
WeWork didn't fall
because of one man's ego.

166
00:08:51,847 --> 00:08:55,190
It fell because it rented for
years and sold by the month.

167
00:08:55,257 --> 00:09:00,067
And because the investor paying for the
growth was also the one who set the price.

168
00:09:00,497 --> 00:09:04,872
The founder survived for a simpler
reason: a valuation isn't cash,

169
00:09:04,939 --> 00:09:07,497
and a famous name opens the next door.

170
00:09:07,927 --> 00:09:11,984
So here's the lesson. When a
founder story quotes a valuation,

171
00:09:12,051 --> 00:09:15,777
ask who set that price,
and how much cash changed hands.

172
00:09:16,197 --> 00:09:22,757
Flow says it may go public one day, and if
it does, it will have to file its own S-1,

173
00:09:22,824 --> 00:09:25,780
with every number, for anyone to read.

174
00:09:25,847 --> 00:09:32,022
And for the first time, we'll all finally
get to read the founder's own account,

175
00:09:32,089 --> 00:09:36,927
line by line, in public, the way
the world once read WeWork's.

176
00:09:37,357 --> 00:09:42,177
Should a founder get a second chance
this fast? Tell us in the comments below.

